course: biology
lesson: 2
title: The rainforest from the top: emergent layer and canopy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A tropical rainforest is a tower of homes in layers: a few giant emergent trees poke out at the very top, below them the canopy, a thick roof of leaves 30 to 45 metres up, then the dim understorey and the dark forest floor.
explain: From a helicopter the canopy looks like a green sea of broccoli, and that roof is where most rainforest animals live, because that is where the sunlight, the fruit and the fresh leaves are.
explain: Rainforests lie near the equator, where the sun is strong all year, so on a holiday in Malaysia the hot morning air rises, cools into storm clouds and pours down almost every afternoon.
explain: Up in the canopy every animal has its own trick: the sloth hangs still, the gibbon swings, the toucan reaches with its beak and the harpy eagle waits on the tallest tree above them all.
explain: In The Jungle Book, King Louie the orangutan rules a ruined temple in an Indian jungle; real orangutans live only on the islands of Borneo and Sumatra, and they are quiet, mostly solitary apes, not kings of a monkey gang.
fact: The canopy is the thick upper layer of leaves and branches about 30 to 45 metres above the ground, and the emergent trees stick up above it.
fact: Rainforests cover only about 6 per cent of Earth's land, but scientists think more than half of all land plant and animal species live in them.
fact: The harpy eagle perches on emergent trees and swoops into the canopy for sloths and monkeys; it can weigh up to about 9 kg, with talons around 13 cm long, about as long as a grizzly bear's claws.
fact: Three-toed sloths hang by long curved claws, eat leaves that give little energy, move only a few metres a minute, which saves energy and helps them hide from eagles, and climb down about once a week to go to the toilet.
fact: Green algae grow in a sloth's fur and help to hide it among the leaves.
fact: Howler monkeys roar at dawn to tell other groups where their territory is; a large hollow bone in the throat makes the boom, which can carry about 5 km, making them some of the loudest land animals.
fact: A toucan's huge beak is light because it is a honeycomb of bone covered with keratin; it reaches fruit on thin twigs and also lets the bird lose heat, like a radiator.
fact: Orangutans are the largest tree-living animals, build a fresh sleeping nest of bent branches almost every night and live only on Borneo and Sumatra.
fact: The blue morpho's blue is not paint: tiny ridges on its wing scales bounce back only blue light, so the colour would vanish if the scales were ground to powder.
fact: Bromeliads grow on branches and catch rainwater in a cup of leaves, a tiny pond for insects, and some poison dart frogs carry their tadpoles up and drop each one into its own little pool.
fact: Scarlet macaws fly in pairs that stay together for many years, nest in tree holes and eat clay from riverbanks, which scientists think gives them scarce salt.
fact: A strangler fig starts from a seed dropped high on a tree by a bird, sends roots down to the ground and wraps the host, which may die and rot away inside it.
fact: Gibbons are small apes with very long arms and no tail; they swing arm over arm through the canopy, which is called brachiation; they are reported to reach about 55 km per hour and leap up to about 10 metres.
fact: Trees release so much water from their leaves that the Amazon makes much of its own rain, so cutting down the forest can make the region drier.
exercise: What is the canopy of a rainforest? | The thick roof of leaves and branches about 30 to 45 metres up | The dark, bare ground under the trees, where little sunlight ever reaches | The giant trees that stick out above everything | The wide brown river that runs right through the forest
exercise: What are emergent trees? | Giant trees that stick out above the canopy | Young trees just sprouting on the forest floor | Trees that grow under the water of flooded rivers | Trees that have fallen and are slowly rotting
exercise: About how much of Earth's land do rainforests cover? | About 6 per cent | About half of the land | About a quarter of it | Almost all of the land
exercise: Where does the harpy eagle wait for its prey? | On the tallest emergent trees above the canopy | In a burrow on the forest floor | On the bank of a river, standing still like a heron | High in the sky, circling all day over the treetops
exercise: Why does a three-toed sloth move so slowly? | Leaves give little energy, and keeping still helps it hide | Its legs are broken from hanging upside down all its life | It is always half asleep from the heat | It is too heavy for its thin arms to lift quickly
exercise: Why does a sloth's fur look green? | Green algae grow in it and help hide the sloth | It dyes its fur green by rolling in fresh leaves | Its hair is grass green right from the day it is born | It is mould that grows from never washing in rain
exercise: Why do howler monkeys roar at dawn? | To tell other groups where their territory is | To wake up the sun | Because they are hungry and want their breakfast | To scare away the harpy eagle before it can hunt
exercise: How can a toucan carry such a huge beak? | It is a light honeycomb of bone covered by keratin | It is hollow like a balloon and full of warm air | It is made of soft feathers | It rests the beak on branches all day long so it never gets tired
exercise: What does an orangutan build almost every evening? | A fresh sleeping nest of bent branches high in a tree | A hut of mud on the forest floor | A raft to float down the river | A tunnel under the roots of a giant tree, lined with soft moss
exercise: Where do orangutans live in the wild? | Only on the islands of Borneo and Sumatra | All over India and Africa, in many forests | In every single rainforest anywhere in the world | In the Amazon rainforest of South America
exercise: Why does a blue morpho look blue? | Tiny ridges on its scales reflect only blue light | Its wings are full of blue paint | It drinks blue flower nectar | Its wings are see-through and show the blue sky above it
exercise: What lives in the little pools inside bromeliads? | Tiny frogs, tadpoles and insects | Big fish washed up from the river | Nothing, the water is poisonous | Baby crocodiles
exercise: Why do scarlet macaws eat clay from riverbanks? | Scientists think it gives them salt that is scarce in the rainforest | They are building their nests out of it, high up in the trees | They mistake it for chocolate | They have no beaks to eat fruit, so they must lick soft clay instead
exercise: How does a strangler fig begin its life? | As a seed dropped high on a host tree by a bird | As a seed buried in the forest floor by a monkey | As a branch broken off another fig tree by the wind | As a thin vine that climbs up from the river bank
exercise: How do gibbons travel through the canopy? | By swinging arm over arm from branch to branch | By gliding on flaps of skin | By jumping from tree to tree using only their legs | By walking along the ground and climbing up
exercise: Why does it rain almost every afternoon in many rainforests? | The strong sun heats damp air, which rises, cools and forms storm clouds | The sea washes over the forest each afternoon at high tide, and then flows away | The trees shake water off their leaves | Clouds always come from the mountains at 3 o'clock
test: «King Louie's an orangutan in India in the Jungle Book so they live in India yeah» What is right? | No, wild orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra | Yes, India is their main home, as in the film | Yes, and they also rule old temples there, just like King Louie | No, they live only in Africa
test: «ugh sloths are so lazy, just get a move on» Which is the best answer? | Their leaf food gives little energy, and moving slowly saves it and hides them | Sloths are ill, healthy ones run fast | Sloths move slowly because they are very old, and the young ones run about all day | They are fast at night when we do not see them
test: What would happen to the frogs of a tree if every bromeliad on it were cut off? | Some frogs would lose the pools where their tadpoles grow | Nothing, the tadpoles would live in the soil | The frogs would grow bigger without them | The tadpoles would move into the toucan's beak and grow up there
test: «my bro reckons the morpho's covered in blue powder like paint» What is right? | No, its blue comes from the shape of tiny ridges on the scales | Yes, it is blue powder that rubs off on your fingers | Yes, it gets the paint from blue berries that it eats every day | No, it is blue because it reflects the sky
test: «the toucan's beak must weigh a ton, how does it fly lol» Which is the best answer? | The beak is a light honeycomb of bone inside a thin keratin cover | The toucan cannot fly at all, it only hops | It is solid but the toucan is very strong | The beak is full of air like a balloon, so it lifts the toucan up as it flies
test: «howler monkey's dead loud, like a lion or what» Which is closest to the truth? | It is one of the loudest land animals, heard about 5 km away | It is quieter than a cat | It is the loudest animal in the whole sea, heard 500 km away | It is loud only when it is hurt
test: In a Tarzan cartoon, an eagle is shown flying over an empty, sunny forest floor. Where would a real harpy eagle hunt? | Through the canopy, swooping from the tallest trees | Only in open grassland, far from trees | Only on the ground among roots | Over the sea, catching fish
test: «we cut down the forest and the rain'll still come, it's the tropics innit» What is the clever answer? | The trees make much of the rain, so cutting them can make it drier | Yes, rain never changes in the tropics | Cutting trees makes it rain more | Rain comes only from the sea, so the trees do not matter at all for it
test: How many toes are on the front feet of a three-toed sloth, both feet together? | 6 | 3 | 4 | 9
test: «gibbon's a monkey, it's got a long tail for swinging, right» What is right? | No, a gibbon is a small ape with no tail; it swings with its long arms | Yes, it swings by its long tail | Yes, it is a monkey with a short tail | No, it is a kind of sloth
test: «that fig tree's eating the other tree?? that's well creepy» What is right? | The fig grows round its host from the top down, and the host may die inside | The fig eats wood with its roots like teeth | Nothing happens to the host tree at all | The two trees join and become one kind of tree
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the rainforest canopy? | The canopy constitutes the dense upper stratum of foliage and branches, situated approximately thirty to forty-five metres above the ground | The canopy constitutes the forest floor beneath the trees, upon which little light falls and few plants are able to grow | The canopy constitutes the isolated giant trees projecting above all others | The canopy constitutes the river system traversing the forest
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the three-toed sloth moves with such deliberation. | Its diet of foliage yields little energy, and stillness furthermore assists its concealment | Its limbs are habitually injured by the prolonged suspension of the body from branches | It is perpetually asleep throughout daylight | It is incapable of perceiving its surroundings
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the distribution of orangutans in the wild. | Orangutans are confined exclusively to the islands of Borneo and Sumatra | Orangutans inhabit the forests of the Indian subcontinent | Orangutans inhabit every tropical rainforest upon the Earth | Orangutans are native to the Amazon basin
exam: Ascertain the origin of the blue colouration exhibited by the wings of the blue morpho. | Minute ridges upon the wing scales reflect blue light exclusively | A blue pigment derived from the nectar of flowers | The reflection of the sky upon transparent wings | A blue powder deposited upon the wings by rainfall
